CANNOT BE COMPLETED WITHOUT A BUILD, and here is exactly why, so the next attempt is one pass rather than three.

WHAT THE ROW ACTUALLY IS. NEGATIVE_EVIDENCE.md:20905 uses 'batch-parallel bit-parallel grid/1600 6.16ms >> ORIG rayon 1.63ms' to justify RESTRICTING bit-parallel to n < 500 inside that same change. Read in place, that citation is legitimate — it scopes one change on its own measurement. The over-citation the 2026-07-10 audit flagged is the SEPARATE use of it as a general 'bit-parallel loses at n >= 500', i.e. against CHUNKED designs (rayon over source-chunks, bit-parallel within). Those are different claims about different designs, which is precisely why the audit marked it VOID-ZEROSELF rather than wrong.

WHY I COULD NOT RE-MEASURE IT TODAY, despite a quiet host (runq 6, idle 89 pct, loadavg 4.79, no build needed for ordinary aspl timing): the two arms are not selectable from Python. aspl_bitpar_run and aspl_bitpar_run_parallel are private fns in crates/fnx-algorithms/src/lib.rs:11615/11637 with no env knob and no pyo3 binding — grep for a Python-side selector finds nothing. Forcing the batch-parallel arm therefore requires either adding a mode knob or A/B-ing two builds, and /data is at 37G under an absolute no-cargo throttle.

WHAT THE RE-MEASUREMENT NEEDS, when builds resume:
  * a way to force the arm — an env knob read at the aspl entry is cheapest, and it is also what makes this row re-checkable later instead of a one-off;
  * a SELF-TIME figure, not a whole-job ratio: that is the entire reason the row is VOID-ZEROSELF. Time the kernel, not average_shortest_path_length end to end;
  * grid/1600 specifically, since the claim is about n >= 500 where the per-source path fans out;
  * the same-invocation A/B rule, because this ledger already records rch worker drift of 2.02x on IDENTICAL code between two invocations — larger than most effects anyone would be trying to detect here.

NOT RE-MEASURED, NOT RE-CITED, ROW UNCHANGED.

