FOUND BY AN EXISTING TEST, FIX IS IN THE WORKING TREE, NOT YET COMMITTED (CrimsonBeaver, 2026-08-14).

SYMPTOM: tests/python/test_view_descriptor_parity.py::test_direct_edge_attribute_self_reference_is_stored_and_collectable[Graph] FAILED on a build carrying the in-flight edge_py_attrs_by_endpoint lookaside cache. Full suite on that build: 1 failed / 55104 passed. The same test passed on the immediately preceding build (55097 passed / 0 failed), which had every other change but not the cache.

CAUSE, read from source and confirmed by the fix: crates/fnx-python/src/lib.rs. The new cache is a nested HashMap<String, HashMap<String, Py<PyDict>>> holding a SECOND strong reference to every edge's attribute dict (materialize_edge_py_attrs inserts attrs.clone_ref(py) into it). clear_python_refs DOES clear it. traverse_python_refs did NOT visit it.

CPython requires tp_traverse and tp_clear to cover the same references. A reference that is cleared but never traversed is invisible to the cycle collector, so a cycle running through it is never detected and the objects leak. An edge attr dict that references itself is exactly such a cycle, which is why that specific test caught it and nothing else did.

FIX (in the working tree, uncommitted, in traverse_python_refs immediately after the edge_py_attrs loop):

    for row in self.edge_py_attrs_by_endpoint.values() {
        for attrs in row.values() {
            visit.call(attrs)?;
        }
    }

VERIFIED: with the fix, tests/python/test_view_descriptor_parity.py + tests/python/test_view_borrowed_canonical_key_parity.py = 340 passed, including the previously failing case.

WHY IT IS NOT COMMITTED BY ME: the fix lives in lib.rs alongside the cache's own field declaration and lookup logic, which are another agent's in-flight work. Committing the file would put their unfinished design in history under my name. Whoever owns edge_py_attrs_by_endpoint should commit the cache, its 25 initializers (also completed in the working tree) and this traverse fix as ONE unit. If the cache is abandoned instead, this bead closes with it.

GENERAL RULE WORTH KEEPING: any new field on PyGraph/PyDiGraph/PyMultiGraph/PyMultiDiGraph that holds a Py<...> must be added to traverse_python_refs and clear_python_refs TOGETHER. The asymmetry is silent — it costs nothing at the call site and only shows up as a leak under a cycle. Related to the same-shaped requirement noted on br-r37-c1-5gam7 for view GC.

CLOSING PROBE: the named test above must pass on a build containing the cache.

