THE FIX IS NOW SPECIFIED AND VERIFIED WITHOUT A BUILD (GoldenBison, 2026-08-16). /data is at 56G,
below the 58-59G floor, so no build was started and none was needed to derive this. Whoever picks it
up can apply it mechanically and should need only one remote cargo test.

THE GUARD, derived and checked against CPython's builtin sum on the cases that matter:

    let t = f + x;
    if !t.is_finite() {
        // CPython drops the compensation once the running total leaves the
        // finite range: (inf - inf) is NaN and poisons f + c at the end.
        f = t;
        c = 0.0;
    } else if f.abs() >= x.abs() {
        c += (f - t) + x;
        f = t;
    } else {
        c += (x - t) + f;
        f = t;
    }

VERIFIED EQUIVALENT TO builtins.sum, 9 cases, guarded vs raw vs CPython:

    case                     builtin sum   neumaier raw   neumaier+guard
    single inf                       inf            nan              inf   MATCH
    single -inf                     -inf            nan             -inf   MATCH
    inf then 1                       inf            nan              inf   MATCH
    1e308 + 1e308 (overflow)         inf            nan              inf   MATCH
    inf + -inf                       nan            nan              nan   MATCH
    single nan                       nan            nan              nan   MATCH
    [1.0, 1e100, 1.0, -1e100]        2.0            2.0              2.0   MATCH
    [0.1] * 10                       1.0            1.0              1.0   MATCH
    [1e16, 1.0, -1e16]               1.0            1.0              1.0   MATCH

The last three are the ones that matter for NOT regressing: they are the cases where compensation is
load-bearing, and the guard leaves them untouched because it only fires when the running total is
non-finite, at which point compensation is meaningless anyway.

ALL 18 SITES, so nobody has to re-find them. Same 7-line shape everywhere, differing only in
accumulator names (f/c, sf/sc, pf/pc, fo/co, fi/ci) and indentation:

    crates/fnx-python/src/lib.rs      11839, 11847, 11906, 11914, 15994, 16184
    crates/fnx-python/src/digraph.rs  1664, 1684, 1725, 1746, 1819, 1837, 1878, 1895,
                                      9340, 9363, 14393, 14413

RECOMMENDED SHAPE: a single pub(crate) helper -- neumaier_add(&mut total, &mut comp, x) -- in lib.rs,
called from all 18. A numeric kernel with 18 copies of an accumulator is exactly where a partial fix
breeds inconsistency, and a shared helper also gives one place to unit-test the table above.

COORDINATION NOTE: lib.rs was under an active reservation by br-r37-c1-770z8 when I looked, so
whoever takes this should check Agent Mail first -- 6 of the 18 sites are in that file.

STILL PINNED meanwhile by tests/python/test_weighted_degree_nonfinite_parity.py, which fails the
moment this lands so the fix cannot go unnoticed.

ANSWER TO THE QUESTION THIS BEAD WAS NARROWED TO — AND IT DISCONFIRMS MY OWN CANDIDATE (OliveDesert, 2026-08-08). Read from source, consistent with the measurement, NOT yet attributed by profiling. Bead stays open.

THE QUESTION: what does a native add_node that changes nothing spend 538 ns on? Measured last comment: no-op 538.2 ns, real insert 596.2 ns, so the insert is 10% and ~90% runs regardless.

MY CANDIDATE WAS THE CGSE DECISION LEDGER, on the grounds that g2nev showed its cost is retention per CALL rather than per mutation, which matches the shape of a costly no-op. Reading PyGraph::add_node (crates/fnx-python/src/lib.rs:12406 — confirmed impl PyGraph, not the PyMultiGraph one at :6740) there is NO ledger call in the function at all. The candidate is disconfirmed for this path.

WHAT THE FUNCTION ACTUALLY DOES ON A NO-OP, in order:

    let canonical = node_key_to_string(py, node_for_adding)?;   // owned String
    let was_new = !self.inner.has_node(&canonical);
    if self.should_store_node_key(&canonical, was_new) {
        self.node_key_map.entry(canonical.clone())              // clone 2
            .or_insert_with(|| node_for_adding.clone().unbind());
    }
    let py_dict = self.node_py_attrs.entry(canonical.clone())   // clone 3
        .or_insert_with(|| PyDict::new(py).unbind());
    ...
    self.inner.add_node_with_attrs(canonical.clone(), rust_attrs);  // clone 4
    log::debug!(target: "franken_networkx", "add_node: {canonical}");
    if was_new { self.node_iter_mirror_insert(py, &canonical)?; }
    self.bump_nodes_seq();

FOUR heap allocations of the SAME canonical key — one owned from node_key_to_string plus three explicit .clone() calls — and three separate map probes that each hash it, on a call that mutates nothing. The `entry(canonical.clone())` pattern allocates the key eagerly whether or not the entry is vacant, which for an existing node is pure waste in both of the two places it appears. `add_node_with_attrs` is also called unconditionally for a node already present.

WHY THIS IS CONSISTENT WITH THE NUMBERS: it is per-CALL, not per-mutation, which is the shape the 538 ns no-op demands. It is also consistent with key type being free on this path (measured two comments ago, 532.9 str against 539.7 int) — the cost is allocating and hashing the key repeatedly, not deriving it, and both key types allocate the same way.

STATUS, STATED CAREFULLY BECAUSE I HAVE MIS-ATTRIBUTED TWICE ON THIS BEAD ALREADY: this is a reading of the source that fits every measurement taken, not a profile. Four allocations plus three hashes of a short String is plausibly a few hundred nanoseconds, but I have not timed the individual clones and I am not claiming a number for them. The in-crate self-time profile should start here — time the function with the three .clone() calls replaced by borrows or by a single reused key — and it is a cheap first experiment because the change is local.

IF IT HOLDS, the lever is ordinary: compute the canonical key once and stop cloning it. Both `entry(canonical.clone())` sites can use the raw-entry or get-then-insert shape that only allocates on the vacant path, and add_node_with_attrs can take the key by value exactly once at the end. That is a same-shape change to the one already shipped four times in the batch collectors (br-r37-c1-uta2n and siblings), where the fix was likewise to stop paying an allocation the common path does not need.

CGSE LEDGER STATUS: still relevant to add_edge_with_attrs, where g2nev measured it, but not visible on add_node. Do not carry my earlier candidate across.

