comments.text:
THE LEVER, WRITTEN OUT — apply when the freeze lifts (GoldenBison, 2026-08-16).

Not placed in crates/ deliberately. An uncompiled .rs there trips the mtime stale-.so guard and
blocks pytest for ALL ~10 agents until someone builds, and I cannot build (disk 41.1 GiB
against a 42 GiB floor). This is committed here instead, applies by hand in minutes, and I
will drop it into crates/ on request if the pytest cost is acceptable.

MECHANISM (measured, isolated, part 3 in the ledger): edge_py_attrs_by_endpoint is
HashMap<String, HashMap<String, Py<PyDict>>>, so every attribute read hashes BOTH full-length
canonical endpoint strings, and every materialise does two to_owned() allocations. Measured
split of G.edges[u,v] against key length: 1.17x growth below the 128-byte buffer (hashing),
a 2.03x STEP across it at canon bytes 128->133 (allocation), then linear growth above.
networkx is flat at 92.6-92.7ns throughout because CPython caches the str hash.

THE PATCH — an INDEX-keyed lookaside alongside the string one. O(1) in key length, no alloc.

1) crates/fnx-python/src/lib.rs, next to edge_py_attrs_by_endpoint (~2361):

    edge_py_attrs_by_index: HashMap<(usize, usize), Py<PyDict>>,
    edge_py_attrs_by_index_seq: u64,

   and in the constructor (~2919): edge_py_attrs_by_index: HashMap::new(),
                                   edge_py_attrs_by_index_seq: 0,

2) The probe and the fill (put beside cached_edge_py_attrs, ~2861):

    pub(crate) fn cached_edge_py_attrs_by_index(
        &mut self, py: Python<'_>, a: usize, b: usize,
    ) -> Option<Py<PyDict>> {
        // NODE indices move on removal, and bump_edges_seq does NOT fire for
        // that - it guards EDGE identity. Guard on nodes_seq exactly as
        // has_edge_node_index_cache already does.
        if self.edge_py_attrs_by_index_seq != self.nodes_seq {
            self.edge_py_attrs_by_index.clear();
            self.edge_py_attrs_by_index_seq = self.nodes_seq;
            return None;
        }
        let key = if a <= b { (a, b) } else { (b, a) };
        self.edge_py_attrs_by_index.get(&key).map(|d| d.clone_ref(py))
    }

    pub(crate) fn remember_edge_py_attrs_by_index(
        &mut self, py: Python<'_>, a: usize, b: usize, attrs: &Py<PyDict>,
    ) {
        if self.edge_py_attrs_by_index_seq != self.nodes_seq {
            self.edge_py_attrs_by_index.clear();
            self.edge_py_attrs_by_index_seq = self.nodes_seq;
        }
        let key = if a <= b { (a, b) } else { (b, a) };
        self.edge_py_attrs_by_index.insert(key, attrs.clone_ref(py));
    }

3) INVALIDATION - both existing sites, or this is a wrong-answer bug:
   bump_edges_seq (~2949)      add: self.edge_py_attrs_by_index.clear();
   clear_python_refs (~2516)   add: self.edge_py_attrs_by_index.clear();

4) GC CORRECTNESS, do not skip. traverse_python_refs (~2479) walks
   edge_py_attrs_by_endpoint because it holds a strong ref per dict. The new map holds the
   same refs, so it needs the same treatment or a self-referencing attr dict becomes
   uncollectable:

    for attrs in self.edge_py_attrs_by_index.values() { visit.call(attrs)?; }

   CPython requires tp_traverse and tp_clear to cover the SAME references, and step 3 is the
   tp_clear half.

5) The caller, views.rs EdgeView::__getitem__ (~1022), BEFORE building the ArrayString
   canonicals - mirroring what makes has_edge flat:

    if u_item.is_exact_instance_of::<PyString>() && v_item.is_exact_instance_of::<PyString>() {
        let mut g = self.graph.borrow_mut(py);
        if let (Some(ui), Some(vi)) = (
            g.cached_exact_string_node_index(py, &u_item)?,
            g.cached_exact_string_node_index(py, &v_item)?,
        ) && let Some(attrs) = g.cached_edge_py_attrs_by_index(py, ui, vi) {
            crate::require_hashable_node_key(&v_item)?;
            g.mark_edges_dirty();
            return Ok(attrs);
        }
    }

   Note cached_exact_string_node_index is &self today; it will need &mut self or interior
   mutability for the new map, OR split the probe so only the fill needs &mut.

6) Fill it on the miss path, where materialize_edge_py_attrs already returns the dict.

WHAT IS NOT COVERED and must not be claimed: this touches the SIMPLE-graph EdgeView only.
DiGraph routes through PyDiGraph::get_edge_data and the multigraph classes have their own
keyed storage; those are separate applications of the same idea.

GATE ALREADY LANDED: tests/python/test_edge_attr_lookaside_identity_and_invalidation.py, 50
cases, passing on HEAD. Its NEGATIVE case is the one that kills a naive version - it warms the
lookaside for a late edge, removes an EARLIER node so indices renumber, and re-reads. Without
the nodes_seq guard in step 2 that returns ANOTHER edge's attribute dict. It also pins that
all four accessors return the SAME object (the lookaside serves live dicts, so identity is the
contract) and that a re-added edge does not resurrect the old dict.

description:
## What

Add doc comments to the four MAX_N_* constants in fnx-generators explaining WHY
each specific value was chosen and what would break if they were changed.

## Current Constants (fnx-generators/src/lib.rs lines 13-16)

- MAX_N_GENERIC: 100_000 — general graph generation cap
- MAX_N_STAR: 99_999 (MAX_N_GENERIC - 1) — star graph hub has n edges
- MAX_N_COMPLETE: 2_000 — K_n has n*(n-1)/2 edges; K_2000 = ~2M edges
- MAX_N_GNP: 20_000 — G(n,p) is O(n²); n=20000 → ~200M edge candidates

## Why This Matters

These bounds exist to prevent denial-of-service via adversarial graph generation
(Security Doctrine in AGENTS.md). But they're currently undocumented, which means:
1. A contributor might raise them without understanding memory implications
2. They don't explain the relationship between n and memory (quadratic for complete/GNP)
3. The hardened vs strict mode implications aren't captured

## What To Do

Add comments like:
```rust
/// Maximum nodes for generic generators. Chosen to keep memory under ~1GB
/// for adjacency-list representation with average degree ~10.
const MAX_N_GENERIC: usize = 100_000;

/// Star graph cap. K_{1,n} has exactly n edges, so MAX_N_STAR = MAX_N_GENERIC - 1
/// avoids exceeding the generic edge budget when the hub connects to all others.
const MAX_N_STAR: usize = MAX_N_GENERIC - 1;

/// Complete graph cap. K_n has n*(n-1)/2 edges. K_2000 ≈ 2M edges ≈ 48MB for
/// edge storage alone. Beyond this, memory consumption becomes problematic.
const MAX_N_COMPLETE: usize = 2_000;

/// G(n,p) cap. The naive O(n²) algorithm iterates all n*(n-1)/2 possible edges.
/// At n=20000, that's ~200M iterations — acceptable on modern hardware but any
/// higher risks multi-second generation times even in Rust.
const MAX_N_GNP: usize = 20_000;
```

## Files To Change

- crates/fnx-generators/src/lib.rs (lines 13-16)

## Verification

- cargo check -p fnx-generators (doc comments don't affect compilation)
- No test changes needed

